WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
KARTHIK BALAKRISHNAN:
Hello, everyone.

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.540
In this video, we
plan to talk about how

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:06.030
to use Google Cloud to
build infrastructure

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:08.850
for your website that is
scalable, highly available,

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:10.980
and widely distributed.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.240
Today's presentation
has two parts.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:15.300
The first provides a
high level overview

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.490
of what infrastructure
pieces are involved

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:19.590
and how they fit
with each other.

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:22.530
As a brief introduction, I'm
Karthik, a product manager

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:23.970
within Google Cloud.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:26.040
The second part walks
you through the details

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.680
of how to use gcloud to actually
build the infrastructure

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:32.640
end to end and will be covered
by my colleague Arman Rye.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.660
While there are many
options available,

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:36.840
the key insight we want
to drive is to showcase

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.350
how Google Cloud can be used
as a one-stop shop for building

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:43.170
scalable, highly
available websites that

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:46.320
can be distributed all across
the globe with relatively

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.570
low effort.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:52.410
Here are the products that
we will cover in this video.

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:53.900
Let's start at the
very beginning,

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:55.910
buying and registering a domain.

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:58.340
Until now, this process
was outside Google Cloud.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.080
And it was not very
straightforward

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:02.570
to buy, register,
verify the ownership,

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:05.420
and set up DNS records
so that your customers

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:07.190
can reach your website.

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:09.080
We have recently
launched Google Domains

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:10.910
that is integrated
into Google Cloud

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:13.160
to offer a more
seamless workflow

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:15.800
and to manage your
domain's lifecycle.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:17.240
Cloud Domains
allows you to check

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:20.630
for availability of the
domain name and purchase it.

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:22.280
It will also
register this domain

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.650
and verify the
ownership, Finally,

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:26.960
it supports API so you
can programmatically

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:29.330
manage your domain portfolio.

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:31.550
Once domain has been
registered, the next step

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:33.710
is to create DNS records
so your customers can

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:35.210
visit your website.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:38.930
Cloud DNS offers public DNS
zone setter accessible worldwide

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:41.840
and has high availability
and scalability.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:45.260
We will create a public DNS
zone for the new domain you just

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:47.000
registered in this step.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.070
Cloud DNS also supports DNSSEC.

00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:51.650
And enabling it is very easy.

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:53.780
DNSSEC is used to defend
against techniques

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.550
that hackers use
to direct computers

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:58.160
to rogue websites and servers.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:00.170
We will need to add
DNS records to the zone

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:02.280
before it's reachable
by you customers.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.330
But we will do that
in a different step.

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:09.199
As next steps, we will
create the back ends.

00:02:09.199 --> 00:02:12.740
This represents the actual web
server that runs your website.

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:14.960
We will split this
into two parts.

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:18.920
Use managed instance group
for serving your website.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:20.840
This represents compute
virtual machines

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:24.470
that process incoming
requests and identify

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:26.360
what actions to take.

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:28.820
We use managed instance
groups primarily because you

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:30.750
can control them
as a single entity

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:32.840
and because it is
easy to scale when

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:34.970
the demand for your
website increases, say,

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:36.980
during a holiday season.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:40.010
Managed instance groups
work by using a template

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:41.150
that you create.

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:44.420
The template defines machine
type, boot disk image,

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:47.840
or container image and
other instance properties.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:52.070
Once created, you can either
create individual VMs or groups

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:56.060
of VMs and control them
as a single entity.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:59.120
You can manually set the
size of the number of VMs

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.110
or use autoscaling if
your website is stateless.

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:05.630
The managed instance
group automatic updater

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:08.180
also lets you safely deploy
new versions of software

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:11.720
to your MIG and supports
a flexible range

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:13.820
of rollout scenarios,
such as rolling updates

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:15.320
and Canary updates.

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.600
You can control the speed
and scope of deployment

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:20.360
as well as the level of
disruption to your service.

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:23.840
Finally, you can deploy this
in multiple GCP regions,

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:26.990
increasing your fault
tolerance and availability.

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:30.140
Next, we use Cloud Storage for
static content like images,

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:32.870
backgrounds, videos, and
other portions of the website

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:34.670
that don't change frequently.

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:37.760
Cloud Storage allows you to
store large quantities of data

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:39.560
that can be accessed worldwide.

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:42.170
More importantly, it
offers high availability

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:44.340
with multiple
redundancy options.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:47.990
It also makes it easy to ingest
your media content on the one

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:50.400
end and makes it easy
to distribute over

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.080
a content delivery network like
Cloud CDN or another provider.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:56.568
After creating
back ends, we will

00:03:56.568 --> 00:03:58.110
create the front
end that will handle

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:00.550
the traffic to your website.

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:03.180
Google Cloud HTTPS
load balancing

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:06.210
is a global proxy-based
layer 7 load balancer

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:07.860
that enables you
to run and scale

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:12.570
your services worldwide behind
a single external IP address.

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:15.150
It does much better than
DNS-based load balancing

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:17.010
because there are
no TTLs involved

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:18.779
and the load
balancer can respond

00:04:18.779 --> 00:04:21.420
much quicker to changes
in traffic patterns

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:23.640
or health of the packets.

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:26.400
It also allows you to do
content-based load balancing

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:30.090
using URL maps to select the
appropriate back end service.

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:33.270
Finally, you can run within
Google's premium tier

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:36.690
networking that will offer
cross-regional load balancing

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:38.460
and allowing you
to direct traffic

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:42.790
to the closest healthy
back end that has capacity.

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:45.370
Now it's time to add some
security by adding Cloud

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:47.730
Armor to the incoming traffic.

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:50.190
Cloud Armor sits
at the Google edge

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:53.490
and provides DDoS protection,
along with predefined rules

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:56.760
that defend against cross-site
scripting and SQL injection

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:57.750
attacks.

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:01.530
You can also create custom
IP-based allow- and deny-lists

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:04.470
and prevent malicious users
from consuming your resources.

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:09.080
Here's an overall
picture that shows

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:11.650
how everything is put together.

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:13.690
With that, I hand
it over to Arman,

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:19.210
who will go over the same steps
but in much greater detail.

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:20.530
ARMAN RYE: Thanks, Karthik.

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:21.910
My name is Arman Rye.

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:24.910
And I am a network
specialist at Google Cloud.

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:26.930
For this portion of
the presentation,

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:29.470
I will walk you through
an example deployment

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:31.900
on Google Cloud from
start to finish.

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:34.120
We will go from
ordering a domain

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:36.340
all the way through
verifying that all

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:39.550
the appropriate
features are enabled.

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:40.930
Let's get started.

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:45.740
We will begin our demo with the
domain registration process.

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:48.010
The first step is to check
that the domain you would

00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:50.500
like to purchase is available.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:52.420
Running the
search-domains command

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:56.720
will give you a list of TLDs
and the associated prices.

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:00.070
Even though we have specified
a .com in the search,

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:02.830
the result will give many
TLDs that are available

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:05.320
for purchase.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:06.910
Once you have
decided on the domain

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:10.480
name you would like to purchase
and confirmed it is available,

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:14.870
you need to create a Cloud
DNS zone with the domain name.

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:19.250
This zone is where you will
manage DNS for this domain.

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:21.710
Make sure you set
the DNSSEC state

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:27.210
to on to utilize the
security benefits of DNSSEC.

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:30.680
Next, you will actually
need to order the domain.

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:33.440
The gcloud response from
the registrar command

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:36.350
will prompt you to add
information about your business

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:38.690
and list a point of contact.

00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:41.210
The final step is
to link the DNS zone

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:45.740
that we created in step one to
the domain in Cloud Domains.

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:48.290
This will ensure that the
appropriate name server

00:06:48.290 --> 00:06:51.110
records, as well as the
appropriate DS records,

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:54.210
are added for DNSSEC.

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:56.670
A great feature of
the GCP platform

00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:59.170
is that this step is
automated for you.

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:01.470
So you don't need
to do it yourself.

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:05.490
We strongly encourage customers
to use GCP for both their DNS

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:09.660
and registrar needs due to these
increasing product synergies.

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:11.340
In this demo, we
will be configuring

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:14.190
to back ends, one
for static content

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:17.970
like images, logos, or
videos; and the other

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:22.350
for dynamic content, like
HTML and any APIs your website

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:24.060
relies on.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:26.550
Let's start with
the static content.

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:29.010
First, we create
a storage bucket.

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:31.800
For this bucket, we have
chosen to have it be regional

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:37.520
in our us-east1 location and to
use the STANDARD storage class.

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:41.450
Second, we must add some
content to the storage bucket.

00:07:41.450 --> 00:07:43.040
In a typical
deployment, there are

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:46.880
many options on how to upload
content to a storage bucket.

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:49.970
Please see our documentation
for further information

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:52.190
about how to do so.

00:07:52.190 --> 00:07:55.280
Third, we will update the
bucket permissions to public

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:59.220
so that anyone can access
the contents stored.

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:01.640
You can additionally
leverage IAM permissions

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:04.190
if you would like to restrict
access to the bucket.

00:08:04.190 --> 00:08:07.190
But we will not be
covering that in this demo.

00:08:07.190 --> 00:08:10.580
Now that we have set up our
static content storage bucket,

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:13.490
we will pivot to
dynamic traffic.

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:16.580
Before we can create a
managed instance group,

00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:20.120
we must create a template, which
will be the blueprint for all

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:22.990
of the compute we will utilize.

00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:26.290
There's a lot of metadata
you can add in this template.

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:28.690
In this example, we
have picked a region

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:31.870
for the compute to
be deployed, a VPC

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:34.690
network that my
networking is utilizing,

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:36.940
as well as a
specific subnet which

00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:40.270
I want my compute
instances to utilize.

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:43.270
We have additionally
specified an image

00:08:43.270 --> 00:08:45.970
for the compute and a
simple startup script

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:49.580
to install Apache and
run a basic web server.

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:51.100
Now that we've
created the template

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:53.290
for our dynamic
traffic to use, we

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:57.090
need to instantiate the
actual instance group.

00:08:57.090 --> 00:09:00.270
The first command creates
the instance group using

00:09:00.270 --> 00:09:02.920
the template we just created.

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:06.260
The second command specifies
that we are running our web

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:08.970
application on port 80.

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:12.570
In this demo, we are using HTTP
for the back end of our load

00:09:12.570 --> 00:09:16.650
balancer since all traffic is
on Google's private network.

00:09:16.650 --> 00:09:20.460
If you'd like, you can
additionally run HTTPS traffic

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:23.790
on port 443 for your back ends.

00:09:23.790 --> 00:09:26.520
Before we get into
creating the load balancer,

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:28.830
I want to spend a few
moments discussing

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:30.480
what are the
different components

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:33.570
from a gcloud perspective
that are required.

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:36.040
Starting from the
left in this diagram,

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:40.300
we need a globally
accessible static IP address.

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:44.130
This is the IP address that will
correspond to our load balancer

00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:47.050
and is linked using
a forwarding rule.

00:09:47.050 --> 00:09:50.190
Second, we have the
actual proxy itself.

00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:56.310
We need to create two proxies,
one for HTTP and one for HTTPS.

00:09:56.310 --> 00:10:00.300
The global HTTP load balancer
is a proxy load balancer.

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:02.790
So this step is required.

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:05.310
Third, we need to
input some logic that

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:09.420
defines how traffic is routed
between our dynamic back ends

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:11.430
and our static back ends.

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:14.370
This configuration is
called the URL map.

00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:17.250
Finally, we need to
identify the different back

00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:19.980
ends that we want the
traffic to be sent to,

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:24.000
one for our static bucket and
one for our instance group.

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:25.860
In addition, we
need to configure

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:29.610
whether we'd like to use
Cloud CDN for content caching

00:10:29.610 --> 00:10:32.610
or Cloud Armor for security.

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:35.220
There are two steps we need
to take before we start

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:37.780
configuring the load balancer.

00:10:37.780 --> 00:10:41.400
We need to reserve a static IP
address for our load balancer

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:42.780
to use.

00:10:42.780 --> 00:10:47.790
A common mistake in this step is
not to specify the global flag.

00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:49.530
Once we have
reserved it, we need

00:10:49.530 --> 00:10:53.100
to list it please save
this IP address for later

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:55.110
in the demonstration.

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:59.100
It's worth mentioning that,
as long as this IP address is

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:02.670
in your static IP
addresses, this IP

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:06.150
will be able to be
utilized by your resources

00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:09.590
in your GCP project.

00:11:09.590 --> 00:11:12.040
Additionally, we need to
make sure that the firewall

00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:17.470
in our VPC is set to allow
traffic from the /16 and /22

00:11:17.470 --> 00:11:19.600
[INAUDIBLE] ranges listed.

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:22.810
If you do not do this step your
health checks will be blocked

00:11:22.810 --> 00:11:25.810
and no traffic will be able
to reach your back end.

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:29.570
Now we can get started with
configuring our load balancer.

00:11:29.570 --> 00:11:32.120
In this first command, we
create a basic health check,

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:34.880
which will probe our
back end on port 80.

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:37.790
This command utilizes
the default settings.

00:11:37.790 --> 00:11:40.160
Know that there are a
lot of customizations

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:43.170
available beyond the
defaults for health checks.

00:11:43.170 --> 00:11:46.640
But we will not be covering
them in this session.

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:48.890
Health checks are a
very powerful tool

00:11:48.890 --> 00:11:51.410
which allow for only
healthy back ends

00:11:51.410 --> 00:11:54.920
to receive end user traffic.

00:11:54.920 --> 00:11:57.470
The second command
creates a back end service

00:11:57.470 --> 00:12:00.200
which we will attach
to our back ends too.

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:04.310
You need to attach a health
check to the back end service.

00:12:04.310 --> 00:12:07.370
Once we have created
our back end service,

00:12:07.370 --> 00:12:10.520
we need to add the
back ends to it.

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:13.750
The first command adds our
dynamic back end service

00:12:13.750 --> 00:12:15.840
to the back end.

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:18.240
The second command
creates a back end bucket

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:20.670
for our static content.

00:12:20.670 --> 00:12:23.510
Additionally, we are
including the enable-cdn flag

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:24.980
in our request.

00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:27.590
By turning on the
CDN, we are ensuring

00:12:27.590 --> 00:12:30.380
that static content
is served globally

00:12:30.380 --> 00:12:33.620
from Google's edge
caches instead of direct

00:12:33.620 --> 00:12:35.960
from the bucket every time.

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:38.060
By doing so, we are
greatly increasing

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:40.880
the speed of the website.

00:12:40.880 --> 00:12:44.840
Cloud CDN by default will
cache static file types.

00:12:44.840 --> 00:12:48.050
Google determines file type
by looking at the MIME type

00:12:48.050 --> 00:12:50.360
of the response from origin.

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:53.750
We then mapped the MIME type
to a list of static content

00:12:53.750 --> 00:12:56.090
mappings that we maintain.

00:12:56.090 --> 00:12:59.300
If you do not want to utilize
this method for cache control,

00:12:59.300 --> 00:13:02.810
no problem, you can set your
own cache control headers

00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:03.800
at origin.

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:07.670
Or you can configure a
force cache all setting.

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:09.740
Be careful when
setting the force cache

00:13:09.740 --> 00:13:12.200
all setting because
you may inadvertently

00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:15.530
end up caching content that
is intended to be dynamic,

00:13:15.530 --> 00:13:17.850
i.e. non-cachable.

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:21.480
The third command here adds
a custom response header

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:24.420
to requests for this back end.

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:27.450
The custom response header
indicates whether or not

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:30.990
the request was served
from the CDN cache.

00:13:30.990 --> 00:13:32.790
There are a lot of
other custom headers

00:13:32.790 --> 00:13:36.180
that can be added in
addition to the cache status.

00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:38.640
You can additionally
add your own headers

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:41.720
and set them to be
whatever you'd like.

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:46.040
Once the back ends are created,
we need to create a URL map.

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:48.590
The URL map is a
construct that is

00:13:48.590 --> 00:13:53.410
used to determine traffic
routing between our back ends.

00:13:53.410 --> 00:13:57.160
The first command creates
the URL map object.

00:13:57.160 --> 00:13:59.260
In this command,
we are specifying

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:02.560
that the default location
for traffic to be sent to

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:04.720
is our dynamic back end.

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:08.770
The second command adds the
logic that any traffic destined

00:14:08.770 --> 00:14:14.110
to the path or directory
/images/* should instead be

00:14:14.110 --> 00:14:16.240
sent to the back end bucket.

00:14:16.240 --> 00:14:18.700
You can add many
different paths here.

00:14:18.700 --> 00:14:21.730
But, for our demo, we're
just going to use one.

00:14:21.730 --> 00:14:23.530
Additionally, it's
worth mentioning

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:26.890
that there are many more
advanced routing capabilities

00:14:26.890 --> 00:14:31.270
available to you beyond path
matching in the global HTTPS

00:14:31.270 --> 00:14:32.740
load balancer.

00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:36.850
The full list of attributes that
you can route on in the URL map

00:14:36.850 --> 00:14:40.060
is listed in our documentation,
as well as guidance

00:14:40.060 --> 00:14:42.205
on how to deploy this.

00:14:42.205 --> 00:14:44.970
In this section, we
will create the proxies

00:14:44.970 --> 00:14:48.990
and order an SSL certificate
to be served at our edge.

00:14:48.990 --> 00:14:51.870
The SSL certificate will be
issued by Google certificate

00:14:51.870 --> 00:14:54.450
authority or Let's Encrypt.

00:14:54.450 --> 00:14:56.040
There are a lot
of customizations

00:14:56.040 --> 00:14:58.380
available within SSL GCP.

00:14:58.380 --> 00:15:01.800
You can specify, for
example, which TLS versions

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:04.080
you would like to
support or what

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:07.600
cipher suites you would like
clients to support as well.

00:15:07.600 --> 00:15:11.650
In this example, we're going
to stick with the defaults.

00:15:11.650 --> 00:15:14.920
The first command
creates the HTTP proxy.

00:15:14.920 --> 00:15:17.260
This is linked to
the URL map which we

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:19.810
created in the previous step.

00:15:19.810 --> 00:15:23.340
The second command orders
an SSL certificate.

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:25.650
There are no
validation records we

00:15:25.650 --> 00:15:29.370
need to add since we
are using Cloud DNS.

00:15:29.370 --> 00:15:31.290
And, later, we
will point traffic

00:15:31.290 --> 00:15:34.830
to the static IP assigned
to this load balancer.

00:15:34.830 --> 00:15:38.820
The SSL certificate will take
a few minutes to provision,

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:41.500
but it will be
done automatically.

00:15:41.500 --> 00:15:44.070
We need to make sure,
however, that we specify

00:15:44.070 --> 00:15:46.830
all of the host names
that we want listed here

00:15:46.830 --> 00:15:49.010
in the certificate.

00:15:49.010 --> 00:15:51.740
Even while the SSL
certificate is provisioning,

00:15:51.740 --> 00:15:55.130
we can use this SSL
certificate to attach

00:15:55.130 --> 00:15:57.620
to our HTTPS proxy,
which is what

00:15:57.620 --> 00:16:00.350
we are doing in step three.

00:16:00.350 --> 00:16:02.690
The last step of
the load balancer

00:16:02.690 --> 00:16:08.270
is to attach the static IP
to the HTTP and HTTP proxies.

00:16:08.270 --> 00:16:11.940
Step one attaches the
IP to the HTTP proxy.

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:16.070
And step two attaches the
IP to the HTTPS proxy.

00:16:16.070 --> 00:16:20.000
By doing this, the IP address,
which is announced globally

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:22.760
will be accessible from
the internet on both ports

00:16:22.760 --> 00:16:25.820
80 and 443.

00:16:25.820 --> 00:16:27.950
We aren't covering
it in this demo,

00:16:27.950 --> 00:16:33.830
but you can do an HTTP-to-HTTPS
redirect in the load balancer.

00:16:33.830 --> 00:16:36.980
You can also set
HSTS response headers

00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:40.340
if you would like all future
requests by the same client

00:16:40.340 --> 00:16:43.710
to use HTTPS no matter what.

00:16:43.710 --> 00:16:45.780
One of the last
steps in our demo

00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:49.080
is to create a Google
Cloud Armor policy.

00:16:49.080 --> 00:16:53.010
Cloud Armor is Google's DDoS
and web application firewall

00:16:53.010 --> 00:16:54.770
solution.

00:16:54.770 --> 00:16:58.070
Before we configure it,
I want to quickly explain

00:16:58.070 --> 00:17:01.800
how Cloud Armor works and
how it is provisioned.

00:17:01.800 --> 00:17:04.740
Cloud Armor,
similar to Cloud CDN

00:17:04.740 --> 00:17:09.520
is available as part of the
global HTTPS load balancer.

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:13.060
Cloud Armor consists
of security policies.

00:17:13.060 --> 00:17:17.079
A security policy is
a collection of rules.

00:17:17.079 --> 00:17:21.880
Rules can be custom firewall
rules or particular protections

00:17:21.880 --> 00:17:24.940
included in the OWASP top 10.

00:17:24.940 --> 00:17:28.660
A Cloud Armor rule has a
priority associated with it.

00:17:28.660 --> 00:17:33.190
Similar to the VPC firewall,
the lower the priority number,

00:17:33.190 --> 00:17:38.820
the higher up in the match
hierarchy a given rule is.

00:17:38.820 --> 00:17:43.200
A security policy is attached
to a back-end service.

00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:46.680
A back-end service can only
have one security policy

00:17:46.680 --> 00:17:48.770
associated with it.

00:17:48.770 --> 00:17:51.980
A security policy can
be reused, however,

00:17:51.980 --> 00:17:55.810
across multiple
back-end services.

00:17:55.810 --> 00:17:58.120
To configure Cloud
Armor, we must first

00:17:58.120 --> 00:18:00.820
create a security policy.

00:18:00.820 --> 00:18:03.490
The security policy
will contain the rules

00:18:03.490 --> 00:18:07.630
that are applied to our
dynamic content back end.

00:18:07.630 --> 00:18:10.360
Once the security
policy has been created,

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:13.950
we can then create
an example rule.

00:18:13.950 --> 00:18:16.470
The example rule I
created will block

00:18:16.470 --> 00:18:20.100
any traffic that
contains the HTTP request

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:22.380
header called evil-header.

00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:24.030
I have also specified
that I would

00:18:24.030 --> 00:18:29.240
like to respond to this traffic
with a 403 response code.

00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:33.140
This custom rule utilizes the
Common Expression Language,

00:18:33.140 --> 00:18:34.490
or CEL.

00:18:34.490 --> 00:18:38.120
This is an open-source language
designed for simplicity,

00:18:38.120 --> 00:18:42.890
speed, safety, and portability.

00:18:42.890 --> 00:18:45.470
I want to take this moment
to additionally highlight

00:18:45.470 --> 00:18:48.500
that there is an implicit
rule in Cloud Armor security

00:18:48.500 --> 00:18:53.390
policies which dictates whether
traffic is allowed or blocked

00:18:53.390 --> 00:18:56.790
by default for that
security policy.

00:18:56.790 --> 00:19:00.240
If we do not specify this
flag in the first command,

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:03.600
it will default to
an allow setting.

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:06.180
This translates to
any traffic that

00:19:06.180 --> 00:19:09.390
does not match any of
the rules we can figure

00:19:09.390 --> 00:19:12.300
will be let through.

00:19:12.300 --> 00:19:16.320
Now that we have a security
policy and an example rule,

00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:19.410
the remaining step is to
apply it to a back end.

00:19:19.410 --> 00:19:21.810
The second step here
is an optional one

00:19:21.810 --> 00:19:23.820
but a recommended step.

00:19:23.820 --> 00:19:28.230
It enables Cloud Armor's
adaptive protection feature.

00:19:28.230 --> 00:19:31.680
Adaptive protection builds
machine learning models

00:19:31.680 --> 00:19:35.130
which detect an alert on
anomalous application-level

00:19:35.130 --> 00:19:36.750
activity.

00:19:36.750 --> 00:19:39.090
It additionally
generates a signature

00:19:39.090 --> 00:19:43.410
describing the attack, as well
as generates a custom Google

00:19:43.410 --> 00:19:48.060
WAF rule to block the
detected signature.

00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:51.210
Adaptive protection is
in public preview as

00:19:51.210 --> 00:19:54.170
of the time of this recording.

00:19:54.170 --> 00:19:56.410
It's worth mentioning
as well that the custom

00:19:56.410 --> 00:19:59.950
rule that that adaptive
protection generates

00:19:59.950 --> 00:20:02.890
is not automatically applied.

00:20:02.890 --> 00:20:04.840
There is an alerting mechanism.

00:20:04.840 --> 00:20:07.600
And you as the user can
decide whether or not

00:20:07.600 --> 00:20:10.840
you want to deploy this
rule for your back end.

00:20:10.840 --> 00:20:13.900
Now that everything is
set up, the only task

00:20:13.900 --> 00:20:17.500
we have left to do is
to point DNS to the IP

00:20:17.500 --> 00:20:20.440
we reserved for
our load balancer.

00:20:20.440 --> 00:20:22.990
Earlier in the
presentation, we highlighted

00:20:22.990 --> 00:20:27.110
how to reserve and obtain
this static IP address.

00:20:27.110 --> 00:20:29.360
If you didn't get a
chance to write it down,

00:20:29.360 --> 00:20:32.830
you can always go back
and re-list your static IP

00:20:32.830 --> 00:20:36.540
addresses for your project.

00:20:36.540 --> 00:20:39.840
The DNS gcloud
integration utilizes

00:20:39.840 --> 00:20:42.540
a concept called a transaction.

00:20:42.540 --> 00:20:46.830
To add any new records, you
must begin a transaction.

00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:49.500
You can see this
command in step one

00:20:49.500 --> 00:20:53.990
where we start a
transaction for our zone.

00:20:53.990 --> 00:20:56.210
Once the transaction
has begun, you

00:20:56.210 --> 00:20:59.570
need to specify the records
you would like to add.

00:20:59.570 --> 00:21:02.960
In this example, since we
are only adding two records,

00:21:02.960 --> 00:21:05.990
I've explicitly listed
them individually.

00:21:05.990 --> 00:21:09.140
If you have many records,
you can upload your records

00:21:09.140 --> 00:21:10.850
using a BIND file.

00:21:10.850 --> 00:21:12.410
For this demonstration,
we will be

00:21:12.410 --> 00:21:16.760
adding a www record and
an apex record pointing

00:21:16.760 --> 00:21:20.630
to the static IP address
we configured earlier.

00:21:20.630 --> 00:21:22.240
Now that we have
added the records,

00:21:22.240 --> 00:21:24.490
we can complete our transaction.

00:21:24.490 --> 00:21:28.030
Once the transaction
completes, the change

00:21:28.030 --> 00:21:30.850
then propagates to
our edge and traffic

00:21:30.850 --> 00:21:35.380
will be suitable for the
www and apex domains.

00:21:35.380 --> 00:21:38.870
We have completed the
setup for our demo.

00:21:38.870 --> 00:21:41.390
Now, for a final few checks.

00:21:41.390 --> 00:21:43.570
We can make sure that
everything is functioning

00:21:43.570 --> 00:21:45.880
as it is supposed to.

00:21:45.880 --> 00:21:48.610
The first check is
perhaps the most obvious.

00:21:48.610 --> 00:21:53.080
I'm able to access the image we
uploaded to our storage bucket

00:21:53.080 --> 00:21:56.500
at the URL listed,
www.gcpwebdemo.c

00:21:56.500 --> 00:21:58.000
om/images/snowboarding.JPG.

00:22:02.460 --> 00:22:05.220
This website is
accessible over HTTPS.

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:08.220
I've included a screenshot of
the certificate information

00:22:08.220 --> 00:22:09.690
from my browser.

00:22:09.690 --> 00:22:12.960
No warnings are prompted,
and the correct common name

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:14.550
is listed.

00:22:14.550 --> 00:22:18.950
The certificate is valid
and has not expired.

00:22:18.950 --> 00:22:21.200
Additionally, you could
see that the organization

00:22:21.200 --> 00:22:24.500
is Google Trust Services for
this particular certificate.

00:22:27.780 --> 00:22:31.380
Next, we will verify
that Cloud CDN is running

00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:35.580
and that static content is
being cached at Google's edge

00:22:35.580 --> 00:22:38.100
as we expected it to.

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:41.940
I've sent a curl to an image in
the storage bucket we created,

00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:44.100
the snowboarding.JPG.

00:22:44.100 --> 00:22:47.460
In the response, I can
see the custom response

00:22:47.460 --> 00:22:51.090
header we added,
gcp-cache-status,

00:22:51.090 --> 00:22:52.980
with the value hit.

00:22:52.980 --> 00:22:57.120
This means that the response
was served from a GCP edge cache

00:22:57.120 --> 00:23:00.120
and not from the
bucket directly.

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:04.440
By using Cloud CDN, you're
able to improve origin offload

00:23:04.440 --> 00:23:09.120
and reduce egress costs while
simultaneously increasing

00:23:09.120 --> 00:23:13.120
the response speed
for the end user.

00:23:13.120 --> 00:23:15.490
To test that Cloud
Armor is working,

00:23:15.490 --> 00:23:19.330
we can send a curl request,
including the header name

00:23:19.330 --> 00:23:20.950
evil-header.

00:23:20.950 --> 00:23:23.950
We configured the Cloud
Armor security policy

00:23:23.950 --> 00:23:27.400
with a rule that
explicitly blocks traffic

00:23:27.400 --> 00:23:29.500
with this header.

00:23:29.500 --> 00:23:33.320
The response from the
curl is a 403 forbidden,

00:23:33.320 --> 00:23:35.600
which is exactly
what we expected

00:23:35.600 --> 00:23:39.280
and consistent with
what was configured.

00:23:39.280 --> 00:23:42.770
Thank you for listening to
this presentation and demo.

00:23:42.770 --> 00:23:44.710
We hope you found it useful.

00:23:44.710 --> 00:23:48.340
Please add your questions to
the comments if you have any.

00:23:48.340 --> 00:23:49.850
Thanks.

